Should lsv 'wait' for lsvstart to finish?  Should it just exec
lsvstart and then wait inside lsvstart?  Should it set itself up
as a 'group' so signals get propagated automagically (note that this
is only a win for local processes.  remote processes will have to be
killed some other way anyway).

On the other hand, is there any advantage for lsv to return to the
caller's shell?  It makes it easier to do stuff like debugging in a
single shell window.

The code looks general enough to handle the case of Nproc "elements" and
"Nhost" separate, distinct 'hendrix' servers, running as separate processes,
possibly on remote machines.  Can you say "Scalable I/O"?

There is some very twisted sh code in lsvstart that starts up
remote processes.  I doubt that this is the optimal solution to the
problem of starting up remote processes.  /bin/sh is probably the
wrong 'language'.

lsv.c could be written in perl.  lsvstart could be written in perl.
We still link against udp.c, which wouldn't be in perl, so it's probably
pointless to write lsv in perl.  However, it might be a good idea to add
smarts to a perl-ized version of lsvstart or lsvstart_remote.  There should
be more control over where/how remotes get started than the -m "list" arg.
How?

liblsv consists only of udp.c.  It could easily be swallowed whole by
sysdep/mpmy_lsv.c.  This would eliminate a library and (perhaps) simplify
the Makefiles in Make-common (since they wouldn't have to check for lsv
and modify LOADLIBES).  Since the 'host' program (lsv.c) also links with
udp.c, there might still be some Makefile trickery to keep only one version
of the file.
** Progress:  the old udp.c has been moved to libsw/lsv.c.  It can be
conditionally added to the .a file for a particular ARCH by adding it
to the 'extras.$ARCH' auxiliary Makefile in libsw.  I'm sure we'll discover
OS/ARCH-specific nuances to the #include files, etc. as we add it to
various extras.$ARCH files.  liblsv is now obsolete.


